WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.001
The Texas governor is

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.969
vowing to pardon a convicted murderer

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.838
before he has even been sentenced.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.039
This case is exposing

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:06.673
the conflict

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.508
between two bedrock principles

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.176
of the modern Republican Party.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.412
Namely this one

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.750
we support the right of every law abiding

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.119
American to be able to have a weapon.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.223
And also this one

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:25.191
we have

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.560
God given rights.

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.396
And included in those God

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:30.063
given rights

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.198
is the right to self-defense.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:34.934
To bring you up to speed,

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.369
Garrett Foster was killed

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.904
as he was legally protesting

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:40.106
the death of George Floyd in 2020.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:41.107
At the time,

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.877
Foster was legally carrying an AK 47.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:45.578
Daniel Perry's attorneys

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.380
presented a case for self-defense

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.381
at the trial.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:49.149
Last Friday,

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.151
a jury found him guilty of murder.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.619
The sentencing date for him

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.120
has not even been set yet.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.121
But already, Texas

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:55.755
Governor Greg

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.690
Abbott has tweeted his intention

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.958
to pardon Perry.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:00:59.659
In Texas,

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.127
the governor can only grant a pardon

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.595
after the board of Pardons

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.464
and Paroles makes a recommendation.

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:05.565
Abbott has requested

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.801
an expedited review of this case.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:08.435
I want to note

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:09.569
that we invited the governor

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:10.703
on this show tonight

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.205
or this week to join us.

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:13.073
Now, we are speaking

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.040
to the district attorney

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.109
in this case, Jose Garza, who joins us.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.445
And thank you so much for being here.

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.313
The idea that this sentencing date

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:22.248
has not even been set

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.283
yet and already

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.984
the governor is moving ahead with this.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.252
What's your reaction to that?

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:29.389
Well, Caitlin, my first

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:31.991
thought is about the foster family,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:35.228
about Garrett's parents and his fiancee.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.796
They waited

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.299
so long to have their moment in court.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.534
They waited so long for justice

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.669
for their son.

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:43.870
And justice,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:44.571
I think, feels

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.639
so elusive to them right now.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:47.640
But I want them to know

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.142
that the Travis County district

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.343
attorney's office

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.178
is going to continue to fight

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.646
for a criminal justice system

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:54.814
where everyone is treated

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:57.650
equally, including Garrett Foster.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.020
Have you spoken to the family yet?

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.991
I have had the opportunity to visit

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.759
with the family on multiple occasions.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.962
I had the opportunity to visit with them

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:09.863
today.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.464
I won't speak for them.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.833
I think they will

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.002
have their opportunity to speak

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:17.170
very soon.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.138
But I can only tell you

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.439
that they're heartbroken right now.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.474
Okay, good to know

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.709
that you've spoken to them.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.410
If this pardon goes through,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.912
I think an important part to note

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.714
is that the board's members

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.849
here are appointed by the governor.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.084
He's saying he'll bring it.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.119
If it comes to his desk, he'll sign it.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.654
If this pardon does happen,

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:37.290
do you think it undermines your office?

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.794
Well, simply by making this announcement,

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:43.663
the governor has undermined

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.965
the rule of law in the state of Texas.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.502
And he has made our community less safe.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:52.572
Every single day here in Travis County,

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:54.574
we hold people accountable

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:56.643
who commit acts of gun violence.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:57.544
We are going to continue

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:58.945
to hold people accountable

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.580
who commit acts of gun violence.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.247
And obviously,

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.682
if the governor wants to continue

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.316
to pardon

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.251
people who commit acts of gun violence,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.386
that's up to him.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.621
But there is no doubt in my mind

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.890
that it makes our community less safe.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.659
I want to show a moment of something

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:13.526
that was said on Fox

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:14.694
News in the hours

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:16.229
before We heard

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:17.497
from the governor's office

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:18.898
about what he intended to do.

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:19.732
This is what happened

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:23.169
This is a legal atrocity.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.438
It's so obviously unjust that

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.839
we extended an invitation

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.241
to the sitting governor of Texas,

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:29.075
Greg Abbott,

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.110
to come on this show on Monday.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:32.679
And we wanted to ask

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:34.547
if he was considering a pardon

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.649
for Daniel Perry.

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:37.650
But for some reason,

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.651
Governor Greg Abbott's

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.853
office told us he just can't make it

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:41.387
and that we should

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.589
talk to the attorney general

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:44.724
of Texas, Ken Paxton, instead.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:47.627
So that is Greg Abbott's position.

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.429
There is no right of self-defense

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:50.496
in Texas.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:52.732
Do you think criticism like that

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.200
played a role in the decision

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.903
that Governor Abbott has made here?

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:58.471
Well, you know, Caitlin,

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.974
what makes our criminal justice

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.543
system just is people.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:05.845
It is the fact that in our democracy,

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:08.281
it is the people who have the final word

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:09.983
through their service

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.552
on grand juries, through their service

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:13.653
on juries.

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:15.788
And what the governor has said today,

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.523
what he said over the weekend,

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:19.659
is that those people don't matter.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:21.928
And it is deeply troubling

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.463
that someone like Tucker

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.531
Carlson may have more power

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:26.532
in the state of Texas

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.467
than the people

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.869
who live here and serve in our community.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:31.471
District Attorney

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:32.872
Jose Garza will wait for that

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.173
sentencing date to be set,

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:35.008
see what happens there.

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.042
Thank you for your time tonight.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.512
Thank you, Audie.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:40.013
You know

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:40.647
the district attorney,

00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:41.514
you've spoken with him before.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:43.850
I wonder what you make of the position

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.417
that they're in

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:45.051
and the idea

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.719
that legal experts are saying

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:47.987
if this pardon goes forward,

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:50.456
it does erode the justice system

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.190
in Texas.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:51.924
It's not just

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.826
that Garza is one of a handful

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:54.861
of so-called

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.729
progressive prosecutor leaders

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.599
who had the support of protesters

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.701
in the Black Lives Matter movement.

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:04.437
And so he is very much kind of a part

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:06.272
of having a more kind

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:09.509
of progressive approach to public safety.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:10.410
And what's happening with

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.779
Abbott is a clash of

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:14.714
of the politics here. Right.

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:16.582
The backlash to the BLM movement

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.451
is coming in the form of Abbott

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:19.218
and others.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.520
There was a wave

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.121
of anti-protest legislation

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.257
that came out of 20, 20 and 2021,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.725
including in the state of Iowa,

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:26.959
where they actually created

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.329
a civil shield law for people who

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:32.999
harmed a protester in a protest.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.034
So there is kind of a movement of people

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:35.702
who say

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:37.470
get these guys out the streets,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.038
get these bums out the streets,

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:40.940
we don't want to see this protest.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.675
And Abbott is kind of playing

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:44.444
to that crowd

00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:46.346
with the potential for this pardon.

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:48.448
And that means people like Josie Garza,

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:49.782
who I've talked to about this,

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.951
are facing serious political headwinds

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.053
for the sort of progressive movement

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:56.689
they believe themselves to be a part of.

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:57.690
What do you make of it?

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.358
I mean, given

00:05:58.358 --> 00:05:58.925
what Abbott is

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.493
saying, is his defense about the Stand

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:01.761
Your Ground laws in Texas

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.629
and the that side of this,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.498
why not let the sentencing play out

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:07.433
It just seems to undermine

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.302
the deliberations of 12 jurors

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:10.236
who spent a long time

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:11.204
they spent several hours

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:12.672
deliberating on a unanimous

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:13.806
verdict there.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:14.273
But it really

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:15.575
at the heart of this for context,

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:17.110
if you go back to

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:17.577
there was a guy

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.678
named Harlon Carter

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:19.612
who was living on the border

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.748
in Texas back in the fifties.

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:22.849
There was some racial tension

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:24.283
with Mexicans in nearby.

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:24.650
He ended up

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.119
murdering a 15 year old boy

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.154
with a shotgun, was convicted of murder,

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:30.123
but then released from jail

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:30.923
two years early

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:31.958
because the judge didn't

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.226
explain to the jury

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.661
the proper definition of self-defense.

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.462
At that time.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:37.163
Harlan Carter

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:39.031
went on to be the head of the NRA.

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:39.699
At the time,

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.100
they pivoted from a club

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.002
that was for marksmanship

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:45.037
into a political wing

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.639
that gave their first endorsement

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:47.507
after 100 years

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:50.943
to Ronald Reagan in 1980 and now that's

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:54.781
180 million guns ago in this country.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:57.083
The political force of that decision

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.318
who took the NRA in a place

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.953
to fortify for a race war

00:07:00.953 --> 00:07:02.522
instead of a civic

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:03.122
gun safety

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.124
organization was a key moment in history

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:05.725
and I think you're

00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:08.060
seeing that play out now.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.362
This is like canonizing

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:10.897
the Kyle Rittenhouse's of the world.

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:12.331
How have you feel about what went down

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.500
to undermine just

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.268
the basics

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:17.303
sense of a jury trial at this moment

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:18.671
and to choose sides

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:19.572
in a gunfight

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:21.874
based on who is protesting what?

00:07:22.642 --> 00:07:24.043
Boy, that's dangerous

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:24.811
and it feels like

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:25.878
and it's important to remember

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.747
what was introduced at

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:29.315
the trial were his messages

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:31.451
that were posted that day.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:32.251
One saying, you know,

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:33.586
I might have to kill a few people

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:34.320
on my way to work.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.254
They're writing outside

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:36.722
my apartment complex.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.524
I might go to Dallas to shoot looters.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:40.393
These are the words that prosecutors

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:41.928
used against him in this trial.

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:42.895
Yeah, but I mean, there's

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:44.764
so many absurd things about this.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:46.532
One of them to me there really jumps out

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:47.099
is that Abbott's

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.134
point is that the Stand

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:50.736
Your Ground laws in Texas are so strong,

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:52.538
which I feel like undercuts his logic.

00:07:52.538 --> 00:07:54.140
Yes, they're so strong.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.641
And the dude still got convicted.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:56.476
And I am a Texan,

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:57.477
and I'm here to tell you,

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:58.911
we're really understanding

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:00.079
when you shoot somebody,

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:00.746
we will give you

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:01.848
every benefit of the doubt

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:03.616
that maybe you had a reason to do this.

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:05.618
And will people still unanimously said,

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:06.953
no, this isn't enough.

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:08.521
So with the strength of those laws,

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:10.089
the person still got convicted.

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:11.924
That's a huge part that really throws me.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:12.191
You know,

00:08:12.191 --> 00:08:13.526
when we see how this all went down.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:14.160
But I think we're already

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.361
saying it is very important.

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:17.497
The idea that you can shoot somebody

00:08:17.497 --> 00:08:18.698
just because they're protesting

00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:19.398
seems to be something

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:20.099
that they want to go.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:21.033
But there's also, again,

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:22.935
to get into the irony of this,

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:23.736
the man who got shot

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:24.170
was carrying

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.071
an AK 47,

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:26.372
and the idea was that

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:28.040
the shooter was scared of him, thereby

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:28.441
he shot him.

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:29.909
First of all, the idea that I'm scared,

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:30.743
therefore I can shoot it

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:31.377
so that legally

00:08:31.377 --> 00:08:32.245
we really got to find a way

00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:33.045
to get to the bottom of it

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:33.779
because being scared

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:34.614
isn't a good enough reason

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:35.515
to shoot somebody.

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:38.284
But if the AK 47 means by definition

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:39.318
it is scary to somebody

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:40.920
and then they can shoot you.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.088
It would then raise the question

00:08:42.088 --> 00:08:42.955
about whether you should be able

00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:44.624
to have the AK 47.

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:45.992
But we're not going to do that either

00:08:45.992 --> 00:08:47.126
because again, Texas

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:48.060
so it all comes again

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.595
this jumble that doesn't make any sense.

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:51.030
It feels very much like

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:51.364
being watched

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:52.465
Tucker Carlson or somebody

00:08:52.465 --> 00:08:53.699
watching and gave him a call and said,

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:55.134
Hey, Tucker, talking about you.

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:55.668
Oh, well,

00:08:55.668 --> 00:08:57.203
I guess we need to at least act like

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.538
we're going to do something about this.

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:00.106
So all of this is done.

00:09:00.106 --> 00:09:02.275
And I guess for larger, like national

00:09:02.275 --> 00:09:03.709
type stuff, because in Texas,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:05.545
the right

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:06.312
of Texans to shoot

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:08.381
people will never, ever

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:09.515
be infringed upon.

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:10.082
Period.

00:09:10.082 --> 00:09:10.283
Well,

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:11.684
and we've seen how even Governor

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:13.286
Abbott has changed the gun laws

00:09:13.286 --> 00:09:15.154
since all of this happened.

00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:15.922
We'll see what happens

00:09:15.922 --> 00:09:17.523
when that sentencing date does come down.

